I don't think things are turning out very well for our housewives and Armand.

50
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:12,240
Apparently, the police officer is not going to make it to the next episode.

51
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:13,960
Uh-oh.

52
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:14,960
All right.

53
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:20,720
So, we saw some really great vocabulary and even though it's kind of a scary story, there is a lot of things we can learn.

54
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:23,020
So why don't we take a look at it now in Language Takeaway.

55
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:25,940
Language Takeaway.

56
00:03:27,370 --> 00:03:36,430
Well, we're going to look at five words and phrases here and the first one, they described this woman as a kind of creature.

57
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:37,720
Creature.

58
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:38,720
Creature.

59
00:03:39,290 --> 00:03:39,630
All right.

60
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:41,210
So, what exactly is a creature?

61
00:03:41,690 --> 00:03:43,090
Well, a creature can be any animal.

62
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:43,890
Any animal?

63
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:48,250
Yeah, like from a mouse to a bird to an insect or a bug.

64
00:03:48,510 --> 00:03:50,330
So I can say a bird is a flying creature.

65
00:03:50,670 --> 00:03:50,970
Exactly.

66
00:03:51,370 --> 00:03:51,630
All right.

67
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:57,360
You can also use it to mean an unknown and scary animal.

68
00:03:57,360 --> 00:03:59,410
So there's a creature under my bed.

69
00:03:59,840 --> 00:03:59,990
Yes.

70
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:03,070
Or some type of creature was attacking me.

71
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:04,490
Okay.

72
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:09,080
So, a creature can be any sort of living thing or animal.

73
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:10,080
Exactly.

74
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:11,080
Yep.

75
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:12,080
All right.

76
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:13,080
What about our next one?

77
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:14,080
Well, the creature broke free from some ropes.

78
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:14,440
All right.

79
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:15,850
So, to break free.

80
00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:16,830
Break free.

81
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:18,560
Uh-huh.

82
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:18,760
And what does that mean?

83
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:23,170
When you break free, you escape from something that stops you moving.

84
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:23,990
Okay.

85
00:04:23,990 --> 00:04:26,900
So, you have the ability to move again.

86
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:28,160
Yes.

87
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:29,140
You break free from maybe ropes.

88
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:30,000
Or chains.

89
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:31,980
Or even a relationship.

90
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:32,800
Okay.

91
00:04:33,080 --> 00:04:36,520
So, I can say, oh, I finally broke free from that awful relationship.

92
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:37,880
Mm-hmm.

93
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:40,280
Now, that's quite interesting the way we can use this in different ways.

94
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:43,040
So why don't we take a look at some examples now?

95
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:45,000
Example one.

96
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:55,000
Somebody tied up the dog, but he is trying to break free.

97
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:55,220
Example two.

98
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:01,650
I'm glad that I broke free from that horrible relationship.

99
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:09,800
Example three.

100
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:12,860
I want to break free from all my daily responsibilities.

101
00:05:16,450 --> 00:05:16,830
Okay.

102
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:18,330
So break free, it's clear now.

103
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:19,680
Mm-hmm.

104
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:21,040
So why don't we move on to our next word?

105
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:24,710
They thought that maybe this creature was a kidnapper.

106
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:25,850
Kidnapper.

107
00:05:26,210 --> 00:05:26,590
Kidnapper.

108
00:05:26,950 --> 00:05:27,350
Okay.

109
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:28,110
So this is?

110
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:29,800
A criminal.

111
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:30,270
So, a kidnapper is a criminal.

112
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:31,800
Yep.

113
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:32,800
All right.

114
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:34,720
And what exactly does a kidnapper do?

115
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:39,360
A kidnapper takes someone to a place and keeps them there against their will.

116
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:40,240
Against their will.

117
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:41,240
With force or violence.

118
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:42,080
All right.

119
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:43,880
So, I guess that makes sense, right?

120
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:45,600
Because the word has kid in it.

121
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:46,920
Yeah.

122
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:47,920
So, it can take children.

123
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:48,920
Yes.

124
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:50,360
But it can be also, you can kidnap an adult.

125
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:51,280
Yes, that's true.

126
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:51,840
All right.

127
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:54,860
So, this word can actually be used in three different ways.

128
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,160
So we saw a kidnapper, right?

129
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:58,280
Okay, that is the person.

130
00:05:58,520 --> 00:05:59,520
Yeah.

131
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:00,620
And the verb, to kidnap.

132
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:01,810
Okay.

133
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:05,040
Or a noun describing an action, a kidnapping.

134
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:06,080
A kidnapping.

135
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:06,740
Very good.

136
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:08,420
Yeah, a kidnapping took place today.

137
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:09,340
Okay.

138
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:11,140
So, very interesting word.

139
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:14,000
And of course, it's illegal to be a kidnapper.

140
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:14,400
Yes.

141
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:18,180
So, our next word is also very serious and it's also a crime, right?

142
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:19,400
That's right.

143
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:19,620
A rapist.

144
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:20,700
A rapist.

145
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:22,400
A rapist.

146
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:23,400
Okay.

147
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:24,400
So, that is a person that...

148
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:26,120
Who forces someone to have sex.

149
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:26,860
Okay.

150
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:28,680
So, again, against their will.

151
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:29,960
Right.

152
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:30,960
Like a kidnapper.

153
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:31,960
Exactly.

154
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:32,960
Okay.

155
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:33,960
And the verb would be...

156
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:34,960
To rape.

157
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:35,960
To rape.

158
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:36,960
To rape someone.

159
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:37,960
Okay.

160
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:40,440
So, on the lighter side, we heard the police officer say something in a very strange way.

161
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:42,530
He said, let me see your eyes, please.

162
00:06:43,010 --> 00:06:43,210
Okay.

163
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:46,640
And this is an interesting phrase.

164
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:49,630
It's a sound made from pushing two words together.

165
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:50,350
Right.

166
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:52,370
So, let me.

167
00:06:52,370 --> 00:06:58,370
Why don't we hear a few more examples of this and see if we can figure out what are the two words.

168
00:07:00,770 --> 00:07:01,630
Example one.

169
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:05,220
Let me have a look inside the car.

170
00:07:08,010 --> 00:07:08,750
Example two.

171
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:12,770
Let me go out tonight, dad.

172
00:07:16,490 --> 00:07:16,720
Example three.

173
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:23,170
Let me think about it and I'll give you an answer tomorrow.

174
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:28,800
So, basically, let me is...

175
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:30,020
Let me.

176
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:30,900
Let me.

177
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:32,520
Yeah.

178
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:33,060
Let me have that pen.

179
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:34,720
Okay.

180
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:34,940
Let me see.

181
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:38,600
Now, we do this also with other words, right?

182
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:39,600
Yeah.

183
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:41,240
Another example would be give me.

184
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:42,240
Give me.

185
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:43,240
Yeah.

186
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:44,240
Give me.

187
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:45,240
Exactly.

188
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:46,240
Okay.

189
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:47,240
So, an interesting thing.

190
00:07:47,240 --> 00:07:47,840
It's not proper English, right?

191
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:49,240
No.

192
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:49,980
You wouldn't really write it this way.

193
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:51,840
Or find it in the dictionary.

194
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:53,880
But it's really common when people speak.

195
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:59,420
And actually, it's more likely that you'll say let me, give me, than let me, give me.

196
00:07:59,700 --> 00:07:59,940
Okay.

197
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:00,780
Very good.

198
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:05,900
So, I think now it's time for us to listen to our dialogue for the second time.

199
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:10,260
And then we'll come back and look at some other phrases that we saw in our dialogue.

200
00:08:16,690 --> 00:08:18,810
What the heck is going on?

201
00:08:19,030 --> 00:08:20,110
Did you see that?

202
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:21,810
What was that thing?

203
00:08:22,170 --> 00:08:23,150
I don't know.

204
00:08:23,710 --> 00:08:25,750
I'm just glad we made it out.

205
00:08:26,350 --> 00:08:28,050
Look, there's a police car.

206
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:29,640
Help!

207
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:30,640
Help!

208
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:31,640
Please stop.

209
00:08:31,640 --> 00:08:33,460
Howdy, ma'am.

210
00:08:33,460 --> 00:08:34,720
What seems to be the problem?

211
00:08:35,260 --> 00:08:36,560
Is this man bothering you?

212
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:38,600
Officer!

213
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:38,860
Officer!

214
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:42,580
There is like a witch creature back there.

215
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:45,500
We tied her up, but she broke free.

216
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:48,060
And she was about to have us for dinner.

217
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,000
Okay.

218
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:50,460
Calm down.

219
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:51,480
Calm down.

220
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:53,560
Let me see your eyes, please.

221
00:08:54,380 --> 00:08:56,020
Have you been drinking tonight, son?

222
00:08:56,590 --> 00:08:57,910
We are telling the truth.

223
00:08:58,750 --> 00:08:59,690
She's in there.

224
00:09:00,410 --> 00:09:08,930
We suspected her of being a kidnapper or rapist, but it turns out she's an alien or something.

225
00:09:09,450 --> 00:09:09,990
Okay.

226
00:09:10,770 --> 00:09:11,250
Calm down.

227
00:09:11,670 --> 00:09:12,210
Calm down.

228
00:09:12,870 --> 00:09:14,470
Let me see your eyes, please.

229
00:09:16,190 --> 00:09:16,450
Seriously.

230
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:20,600
Are you going to help us or not?

231
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:21,600
Okay.

232
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:23,050
Let's go have a look, shall we?

233
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:25,280
Hello?

234
00:09:25,280 --> 00:09:26,220
Is anyone in here?

235
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:27,720
Be careful.

236
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:29,820
She might be hiding.

237
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:32,180
It's perfectly safe.

238
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:33,280
There isn't anyone.

239
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:42,920
We're going to look at two phrases now in Fluency Builder that you might hear when you're talking about a crime.

240
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:45,020
Fluency Builder.

241
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:49,520
So the first phrase that we're going to look at is, what the heck is going on?

242
00:09:49,780 --> 00:09:50,900
What the heck is going on?

243
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:53,320
What the heck is going on?

244
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:54,400
Okay.

245
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:57,200
So this is just asking what's happening, right?

246
00:09:57,360 --> 00:09:58,360
Right.

247
00:09:58,360 --> 00:10:00,640
So this is a very strong way of saying what's happening.

248
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:01,640
Yeah.

249
00:10:01,640 --> 00:10:02,120
What really is happening?

250
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:03,640
Uh-huh.

251
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:04,780
Now, why do they say, what the heck?

252
00:10:05,480 --> 00:10:05,760
What is heck?

253
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:08,980
Well, heck is sort of a polite way of saying hell.

254
00:10:09,260 --> 00:10:09,540
Okay.

255
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:11,680
So instead of saying, what the hell is going on?

256
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:12,860
What the heck is going on?

257
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,640
Now, why do you do this?

258
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:20,000
Because especially in American English, we don't like to talk about religious words.

259
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:26,080
So like hell and God and Jesus, these things are impolite in American English.

260
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:27,080
Right.

261
00:10:27,080 --> 00:10:28,080
You don't want to offend anyone.

262
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:30,960
That's why we would have other words like, oh my gosh.

263
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:32,040
For oh my God.

264
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:33,960
Or geez.

265
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:34,760
Instead of Jesus.

266
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:35,560
Right.

267
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:36,120
Okay.

268
00:10:37,400 --> 00:10:42,120
So that is an interesting thing and of course, it is a little bit of a sensitive topic among some people.

269
00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:45,200
So that's why it's better to just use these synonyms.

270
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:45,680
Yes.

271
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:46,420
Okay.

272
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:48,420
And what about our next phrase?

273
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:51,660
So they suspected her of being a kidnapper.

274
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:52,160
Okay.

275
00:10:53,280 --> 00:10:56,480
So suspected her of being a kidnapper.

276
00:10:56,480 --> 00:10:57,480
Yeah.

277
00:10:57,480 --> 00:11:00,960
So I suspect someone of being something.

278
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:04,480
So I suspect she is an alien.

279
00:11:05,020 --> 00:11:05,240
Right.

280
00:11:05,960 --> 00:11:07,340
I suspect him of being a liar.

281
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:08,140
Okay.

282
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:09,580
So what about to suspect?

283
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:10,720
What does that mean exactly?

284
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:14,760
To think something that is true, but not be 100% sure.

285
00:11:14,980 --> 00:11:18,400
So you can say, I think she is a kidnapper.

286
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:19,680
Yep.

287
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:21,920
Oh, I suspect her of being a kidnapper.

288
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:22,400
Interesting.

289
00:11:23,320 --> 00:11:24,320
Why don't we hear a few more examples?

290
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:30,440
Example one.

291
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:32,840
The police suspected him of being a bank robber.

292
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:36,580
Example two.

293
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:42,160
We suspect our dog is responsible for this mess.

294
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:46,960
Example three.

295
00:11:49,760 --> 00:11:52,650
The neighborhood suspected that our neighbor was selling drugs.

296
00:11:56,020 --> 00:11:56,380
All right.

297
00:11:56,720 --> 00:11:56,900
Very good.

298
00:11:57,720 --> 00:11:58,420
So we've seen a lot of vocab.

299
00:11:58,720 --> 00:11:59,480
We've seen these phrases.

300
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:05,240
So I think it's time for us to listen to our dialogue for the last time, and then we'll come back and talk a little bit more.

301
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:11,360
What the heck is going on?

302
00:12:14,650 --> 00:12:15,250
Did you see that?

303
00:12:15,530 --> 00:12:16,230
What was that thing?

304
00:12:16,610 --> 00:12:17,010
I don't know.

305
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:18,370
I'm just glad we made it out.

306
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:20,960
Look, there's a police car.

307
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:24,720
Help!

308
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:25,720
Help!

309
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:27,760
Please, please stop!

310
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:32,080
Howdy, ma'am.

311
00:12:33,290 --> 00:12:35,010
What, uh, seems to be the problem?

312
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:36,970
Is this man bothering you?

313
00:12:37,600 --> 00:12:39,080
Officer, officer.

314
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:41,250
There is, like, a witch creature back there.

315
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:44,570
We tied her up, but she broke free, and she was about to have us for dinner.

316
00:12:45,510 --> 00:12:47,830
Okay, calm down, calm down.

317
00:12:48,490 --> 00:12:50,210
Let me see your eyes, please.

318
00:12:50,790 --> 00:12:52,530
Have you been drinking tonight, son?

319
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:54,280
We're telling the truth.

320
00:12:55,260 --> 00:12:56,220
She's in there.

321
00:12:56,680 --> 00:13:02,900
We suspected her being a kidnapper or a rapist, but turns out she's an alien or something.

322
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:06,320
Okay, calm down, calm down.

323
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:08,360
Let me see your eyes, please.

324
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:09,760
Ugh, seriously.

325
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:12,020
Are you going to help us out or not?

326
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:13,360
Okay.

327
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:15,580
Let's go have a look, shall we?

328
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:30,680
Hello?

329
00:13:30,680 --> 00:13:44,040
Is anyone in there?

330
00:13:45,580 --> 00:13:46,220
Be careful!

331
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:47,540
She might be hiding!

332
00:13:57,380 --> 00:13:58,940
It's perfectly safe.

333
00:13:59,260 --> 00:13:59,880
There isn't anyone.

334
00:14:00,360 --> 00:14:21,200
You're mil Taking too long.

335
00:14:30,650 --> 00:14:34,690
Okay, well, Marco, we're coming near the end of this series, aren't we?

336
00:14:34,970 --> 00:14:35,430
Yes, exactly.

337
00:14:35,910 --> 00:14:40,730
It's a short series, but what do you think happens or how do you think the series ends?

338
00:14:40,970 --> 00:14:42,550
What is really going on?

339
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:44,670
A mystery here at EnglishPod.

340
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:46,330
So we want to hear from you.

341
00:14:46,570 --> 00:14:47,570
What do you think is going to happen?

342
